If desktop-init.sh says “No such file”

Then Desktop Lite never got installed during the rebuild.



Replace your .devcontainer/devcontainer.json with this exact config:



{
  "name": "My Linux PC",

  "image": "mcr.microsoft.com/devcontainers/base:jammy",

  "features": {
    "ghcr.io/devcontainers/features/desktop-lite:1.2.10": {
      "password": "vscode",
      "webPort": "6080",
      "vncPort": "5901"
    }
  },

  "forwardPorts": [
    6080
  ],

  "portsAttributes": {
    "6080": {
      "label": "Linux Desktop"
    }
  },

  "runArgs": [
    "--init",
    "--shm-size=1g"
  ]
}

desktop-lite 1.2.10 is the current published version, and the feature officially supports current Debian/Ubuntu-based containers. 



Then:



Ctrl + Shift + P



search:



Codespaces: Rebuild Container

Choose:



Full Rebuild

When it comes back, immediately run:



ls -l /usr/local/share/desktop-init.sh

and:



ss -lnt | grep -E "6080|5901"

If the script exists but still refuses to start

Run:



cat /tmp/desktop.log

Paste that output to me. That log should tell us the exact failure, instead of us having to guess whether it's TigerVNC, Fluxbox, noVNC, permissions, or something else.



Right now, though, we're actually close — your GitHub Codespace itself is working; only the graphical desktop service failed to launch.



4. Your Linux PC should now open

At first you'll see something that looks like VS Code in your browser.



That's normal.



The actual remote Linux machine is underneath it.



Open the terminal in Codespaces with:



Ctrl + `

or use:



☰ → Terminal → New Terminal



Run:



uname -a

and:



cat /etc/os-release

You should see your Linux information.

5. Open the graphical desktop

In Codespaces find the PORTS tab.



Look for:



6080    Linux Desktop

Click the globe/open-browser button next to it.



You'll get the noVNC page.



Press:



Connect



Password:



vscode

Now you should be inside a lightweight Fluxbox Linux desktop. GitHub's desktop-lite documentation confirms port 6080 is the browser desktop and vscode is its default password. 

Important Xbox warning

This is still noVNC, so the cursor issue you had with DistroSea might happen again.



If it does, LM Studio will still work. You can do basically everything through the regular Codespaces terminal, which should be much easier on Xbox Edge than streaming an entire desktop.



For what you're doing with the LLM, I recommend using the terminal rather than keeping the Linux desktop open.

6. Add a few normal PC programs

In the Codespaces terminal:



sudo apt update

Then:



sudo apt install -y xterm pcmanfm htop nano wget curl unzip

That gives you things such as:



xterm      terminal window
pcmanfm    graphical file manager
htop       task manager
nano       text editor
wget       downloader
curl       web/download utility
unzip      ZIP files

You can launch the graphical file manager with:



pcmanfm &

It should appear on your Fluxbox desktop.

7. Install LM Studio's headless version

Do this in the normal Codespaces terminal.



Run:



curl -fsSL https://lmstudio.ai/install.sh | bash

This is LM Studio's official Linux installation command for llmster, its headless daemon. llmster is specifically designed for Linux servers/cloud machines where running the normal LM Studio GUI isn't practical. 



When it finishes, run:



lms --help

If the command isn't found immediately, run:



exec bash

then:



lms --help

You should see commands including:



lms get
lms load
lms chat
lms ls
lms ps
lms server



8. Check your free disk space BEFORE downloading

Run:



df -h

Look for the / filesystem.



You want comfortably more than:



~6 GB free

I'd prefer:



10+ GB free

because your model is 4.66 GB and LM Studio needs additional storage.

9. Download Dolphin 3.0

This is the model you picked:



Dolphin 3.0
Llama 3.1
8B
GGUF
Q4_0

The Q4_0 file is currently 4.66 GB. 



Start with:



lms get Dolphin3.0-Llama3.1-8B --gguf --always-show-all-results --always-show-download-options

LM Studio allows you to search from the CLI and choose a specific GGUF quantization. 



When it gives you choices, find:



dphn/Dolphin3.0-Llama3.1-8B-GGUF

Then select:



Q4_0

You want approximately:



4.66 GB

Don't accidentally choose:



Q8_0    8.54 GB
F16     16.1 GB

Those are considerably larger. 

10. Confirm it downloaded

Run:



lms ls

You should see Dolphin.



LM Studio documents lms ls as the command for listing models stored on the machine. 

11. Check how much RAM Dolphin will require

Before loading it, do:



lms ls

Copy the model key shown for Dolphin.



Then run:



lms load --estimate-only MODEL_KEY --context-length 2048 --gpu off

Replace:



MODEL_KEY

with what lms ls showed.



For example, it might resemble:



lms load --estimate-only dphn/Dolphin3.0-Llama3.1-8B-GGUF --context-length 2048 --gpu off

--estimate-only lets LM Studio calculate the expected memory requirement without actually loading the model. 

12. Load Dolphin

Because Codespaces doesn't give us the sort of GPU LM Studio normally benefits from, explicitly use CPU:



lms load MODEL_KEY --gpu off --context-length 2048 --identifier dolphin

The important settings are:



--gpu off

and



--context-length 2048

LM Studio supports both options officially. 

Why only 2048 context?

The model itself supports much larger contexts, but context consumes RAM.



We're trying to make:



Dolphin 8B
+
Codespaces
+
Linux
+
LM Studio

fit comfortably inside 16 GB.



Once you've confirmed it works, you can try:



lms unload --all

then:



lms load MODEL_KEY --gpu off --context-length 4096 --identifier dolphin

13. Talk to Dolphin

This is the fun part.



Run:



lms chat dolphin

You'll get an interactive prompt directly in the terminal.



Type something like:



Hello! Tell me what model you are.

Press Enter.



